---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Section Summary: Data, Input, and Output</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-4" />

        <p class="text-secondary">
          Activities 1.4 &ndash; 1.10. Use this to review, or to fill in a definition you missed.
        </p>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">1.</span> Output and Comments</h2>

          <p>
            A program talks to you through output.
            Comments talk to the next person reading the code, and the computer
            ignores them entirely.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Comment</dt>
            <dd>
            A note in the code that the computer ignores when the program
            runs.
            </dd>
            <dt>Comment out</dt>
            <dd>
            To turn a line of code off by making it a comment, without
            deleting it.
            </dd>
            <dt>Argument</dt>
            <dd>
            A value you put inside the parentheses of a function, such as
            the text you want displayed.
            </dd>
            <dt>Algorithm</dt>
            <dd>
            A step-by-step set of instructions for finishing a task.
            </dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">2.</span> Variables and Data Types</h2>

          <p>
            A variable is a name for a stored
            value. Every value has a type, and the three you use most are
            strings, numbers, and booleans.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Variable</dt>
            <dd>A named place to store a value.</dd>
            <dt>Assignment</dt>
            <dd>Putting a value into a variable.</dd>
            <dt>String</dt>
            <dd>A piece of text, written inside quotes.</dd>
            <dt>Number</dt>
            <dd>A numeric value, whole or decimal, with no quotes.</dd>
            <dt>Boolean</dt>
            <dd>
            A value that is either <CodeSnippet
            code="true"
            lang={"javascript"}
            theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
            inline
            /> or <CodeSnippet
            code="false"
            lang={"javascript"}
            theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
            inline
            />.
            </dd>
            <dt>camelCase</dt>
            <dd>
            A naming style where the first word is lowercase and later
            words are capitalized.
            </dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">3.</span> Arithmetic and Precedence</h2>

          <p>
            Expressions produce values.
            Precedence decides which part is worked out first, and parentheses
            override it.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Operator</dt>
            <dd>A symbol that performs an operation on values.</dd>
            <dt>Expression</dt>
            <dd>
            A piece of code that produces a value, such as{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
            code="8 + 3"
            lang={"javascript"}
            theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
            inline
            />.
            </dd>
            <dt>Remainder (modulus)</dt>
            <dd>What is left over after dividing one number by another.</dd>
            <dt>Operator precedence</dt>
            <dd>The order in which operations are carried out.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">4.</span> p5 Lab 1: The Canvas</h2>

          <p>
            Drawing is arithmetic with a picture
            attached. Every shape needs coordinates, and the origin sits at the
            top left rather than the bottom.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Canvas</dt>
            <dd>The rectangular area a sketch draws into.</dd>
            <dt>Coordinate</dt>
            <dd>
            A pair of numbers, x and y, giving a position on the canvas.
            </dd>
            <dt>Origin</dt>
            <dd>
            The point <CodeSnippet
            code="(0, 0)"
            lang={"txt"}
            theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
            inline
            />, at the top left corner of the canvas.
            </dd>
            <dt>Pixel</dt>
            <dd>
            One dot on the screen. Canvas measurements are counted in
            these.
            </dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">5.</span> Console Input</h2>

          <p>
            Input arrives while the program is running,
            and it always arrives as text &mdash; even when the user types
            digits.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Input</dt>
            <dd>Information a program receives while it is running.</dd>
            <dt>Prompt</dt>
            <dd>The question shown to the user when input is requested.</dd>
            <dt>Return value</dt>
            <dd>
            The value a function hands back, which you can store in a
            variable.
            </dd>
            <dt>Concatenation</dt>
            <dd>Joining two pieces of text end to end.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">6.</span> Type Casting</h2>

          <p>
            Text that looks like a number is still text.
            Casting converts it, and a failed conversion gives you
            <CodeSnippet code="NaN" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline
            /> rather than an error.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Casting</dt>
            <dd>Converting a value from one data type to another.</dd>
            <dt>Type coercion</dt>
            <dd>
            When a language converts a type automatically, without being
            asked.
            </dd>
            <dt>NaN</dt>
            <dd>
            "Not a Number" &mdash; the result of a numeric conversion
            that failed.
            </dd>
            <dt>Silent failure</dt>
            <dd>
            A bug that produces a wrong answer instead of an error
            message.
            </dd>
            <dt>
            <CodeSnippet code="toFixed()" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline />
            </dt>
            <dd>
            Formats a number to a set number of decimal places, and
            returns it as a <b>string</b>.
            </dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">7.</span> p5 Lab 2: Variables That Draw</h2>

          <p>
            Nothing new here &mdash; the
            same variables, used as the numbers a drawing is built from.
            Change one value, and the whole picture follows.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Hard-coded value</dt>
            <dd>
            A number typed directly into the code instead of being stored
            or calculated.
            </dd>
            <dt>Derived value</dt>
            <dd>
            A value worked out from another value rather than typed in.
            </dd>
          </dl>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  dl {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
  dt {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
  }
  dd {
    margin-bottom: 0;
    padding-left: 1rem;
  }
  section {
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }
  code {
    font-size: 1rem;
  }
</style>
